//# Core exception effect labels (#1279 alias bridge, #1344 typed kinds)
//
// ADR-0085 (docs/exception-effect.md). Every spelling check for the core
// exception effect lives here so the migration has ONE replacement boundary.
//
// The label grammar this module recognizes:
//
//   Error                      erased alias (any kind)      -- legacy spelling
//   Exception                  erased alias (any kind)      -- #1279 bridge
//   Exception[IoError]         KINDED (#1344, ADR-0085 Phase 3)
//   <any of the above>::Throw  the operation label of that effect
//
// The KIND is the static type of the thrown payload. Two kinded labels with
// different kinds are different row elements: `Exception[IoError]` neither
// authorizes nor discharges `Exception[ParseError]`.
//
// **`Error` is the ERASED exception row, not `Exception[String]`.** ADR-0085's
// migration section calls `Error` a compatibility alias *of* `Exception[String]`;
// that reading cannot hold while #786 (`throw(KeyInvalid("x"))` under a plain
// `with Error`) is live, because a String-kinded row would reject every
// suberror throw in the existing codebase. So the implementation reads the
// alias as kind-erased -- strictly WEAKER than any kinded label, compatible
// with all of them. See docs/exception-effect.md ("`Error` の意味").
//
// Runtime identity is deliberately NOT split by kind: every spelling lowers to
// the same abortive Wasm exception tag (ADR-0085 defers the per-kind tag / type
// id ABI choice). The kind lives entirely in the checker's row.

/// The bracketed KIND argument of an exception EFFECT label, or "" when the
/// label is an erased spelling (or not an exception label at all).
/// `Exception[IoError]` -> "IoError"; `Error` / `Exception` -> "".
export fn exception_effect_kind(name: String) -> String {
  // (`open` is a reserved word in vibe, hence the suffixed name.)
  let open_at = String::index_of(name, "[")
  if open_at > 0 && String::substring(name, 0, open_at) == "Exception" && String::length(name) > open_at + 2 && String::substring(name, String::length(name) - 1, String::length(name)) == "]" {
    String::substring(name, open_at + 1, String::length(name) - 1)
  } else {
    ""
  }
}

/// True for either erased spelling or a kinded `Exception[K]` of the interim
/// abortive exception effect.
export fn is_exception_effect_name(name: String) -> Bool {
  name == "Error" || name == "Exception" || String::length(exception_effect_kind(name)) > 0
}

/// Canonical checker/codegen row label. The two ERASED spellings collapse to
/// `Error` (the pinned label every existing row and diagnostic uses); a KINDED
/// label is already canonical and must survive verbatim -- collapsing it would
/// erase exactly the information the row check exists to compare.
export fn canonical_exception_effect_name(name: String) -> String {
  if name == "Error" || name == "Exception" {
    "Error"
  } else {
    name
  }
}

/// True for any qualified operation of the exception effect.
export fn is_exception_operation_name(name: String) -> Bool {
  let split = String::index_of(name, "::")
  split > 0 && is_exception_effect_name(String::substring(name, 0, split))
}

/// True for an operation name that lowers to the shared abortive exception tag.
/// Kind-insensitive on purpose: `Exception[IoError]::Throw` and `Error::Throw`
/// are the same runtime transfer, so every codegen/analysis caller of this
/// predicate keeps treating them as one operation.
export fn is_exception_throw_operation(name: String) -> Bool {
  let split = String::index_of(name, "::")
  split > 0 && String::substring(name, split + 2, String::length(name)) == "Throw" && is_exception_effect_name(String::substring(name, 0, split))
}

/// True for the constructor name used by an abortive exception handler arm.
/// `Throw` remains accepted for the pre-existing AST-level codegen callers.
export fn is_exception_throw_handler_name(name: String) -> Bool {
  name == "Throw" || is_exception_throw_operation(name)
}

/// True for a row label naming the exception effect, in either the bare-effect
/// (`Error`, `Exception[E]`) or the qualified-operation (`Error::Throw`) form.
export fn is_exception_label(label: String) -> Bool {
  is_exception_effect_name(label) || is_exception_operation_name(label)
}

/// The exception kind of a row label in EITHER form -- "" for an erased label,
/// and also "" for a label this module does not recognize (callers gate on
/// is_exception_label first).
export fn exception_label_kind(label: String) -> String {
  let split = String::index_of(label, "::")
  if split > 0 {
    exception_effect_kind(String::substring(label, 0, split))
  } else {
    exception_effect_kind(label)
  }
}

/// Does a row element of kind `declared_kind` cover a requirement of kind
/// `required_kind`? "" (erased) is compatible with everything in BOTH
/// directions:
///
/// - declared erased: `with Error` keeps authorizing every throw, which is
///   what makes this whole change purely additive for existing code.
/// - required erased: the throw site could not resolve its payload's static
///   type (see throw_payload_kind, checker_effects.vibe), so demanding an exact
///   match would invent errors from an inference gap rather than from the
///   program. Gradual, like the ground-gating the rest of the checker uses.
export fn exception_kinds_compatible(declared_kind: String, required_kind: String) -> Bool {
  String::length(declared_kind) == 0 || String::length(required_kind) == 0 || declared_kind == required_kind
}

/// Alias- and kind-aware equality for effect-row labels and qualified Throw
/// operations. Two exception labels match when their kinds are compatible.
export fn exception_effect_labels_equal(left: String, right: String) -> Bool {
  if left == right {
    true
  } else if is_exception_label(left) && is_exception_label(right) {
    exception_kinds_compatible(exception_label_kind(left), exception_label_kind(right))
  } else {
    false
  }
}

/// The row label a `throw` of payload kind `kind` requires.
///
/// An empty (unresolved) kind keeps the erased `Error::Throw` spelling, so the
/// pinned diagnostic text for the pervasive un-annotated throw sites is
/// unchanged. A resolved kind produces the BARE effect label `Exception[K]`
/// rather than an operation label: ADR-0071's operation granularity exists so
/// a fix-it grants one operation instead of a whole effect, and `Exception[E]`
/// declares exactly one operation, so there is nothing to narrow. It also has
/// to be a spelling the user can actually paste into a row -- the row grammar
/// (collect_row_item_targs, parser_base.vibe) accepts `Eff::Op` and `Eff[T]`
/// but not `Eff[T]::Op`, so a `Exception[IoError]::Throw` hint would name a
/// label that does not parse.
export fn exception_throw_label_for_kind(kind: String) -> String {
  if String::length(kind) == 0 {
    "Error::Throw"
  } else {
    String::concat("Exception[", String::concat(kind, "]"))
  }
}
